title: Don't Buy This Jacket
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A full-page ad Patagonia ran in the //New York Times// on Black Friday, November 25, 2011. The headline sat over a photo of one of its own fleece jackets.

The small print listed what that jacket had cost:

* 135 liters of water, "enough to meet the daily needs (three glasses a day) of 45 people"
* nearly 20 pounds of carbon dioxide, 24 times the jacket's weight
* two-thirds of its own weight in waste, left behind on the way to the warehouse

Then it asked people to buy only what they need, repair it, reuse it, and recycle the rest.

A company naming the gap in its own product, on the biggest shopping day of the year. Sales reportedly went up afterward, which is its own question.

See [[Does a sign become more honest when its maker admits what it doesn't yet do?]], [[Greenwashing]], [[TOOOT]]

